  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 315 PM Saturday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Another Heat Advisory has been issued for parts of \nthe Triangle and south central NC for Sun. The hot weather will \ncontinue into mid week, peaking on Monday when most of the area will \nhave heat index values near or over 105F, and our southeast could \nsee 110F or higher. \n\nStrong heating across southern portions of central NC along with \nhigh dewpoints have pushed heat index values to around 105F today, \nwhile over NE and far N sections, this morning's sct-bkn stratus \ncurbed heating a bit, keeping heat indices in the 90s to around \n100F. These clouds were along and NE of a surface boundary evident \nin recent analyses stretching over NW into E NC, with slightly more \nstable air noted earlier today NE of this boundary. This boundary \nshould wash out and dissipate late tonight, putting us back into a \nlight flow from the SSE to SW Sun and pushing high temps well into \nthe 90s most areas. Despite some modest mixing of surface dewpoints \nSun afternoon, readings should still hold in the low-mid 70s, with \nsome upper 70s persisting over parts of the Coastal Plain, and this \nwill result in heat index values peaking around 105 to 109 over much \nof our southern half and Triangle region, where heat island effects \ncould further boost local temps. We've issued another Heat Advisory \nfor Sun for this area. But there is some uncertainty with this, as \nour confidence is increasing that we'll have areas of low stratus \naround the area Sun morning, not dissipating substantially until \nlate morning or the noon hour, which could put a damper on strong \nheating in some areas. If this occurs, heat index values could be a \nbit lower than expected, although this could be balanced by less \nmixing of surface dewpoints. Otherwise, thicknesses are projected to \nbe around 10 m above normal, so highs well into the 90s remain \nplausible. \n\nThis hot stretch is likely to be its worst on Mon, when highs are \nexpected to reach the mid 90s to around 100F areawide, with elevated \ndewpoints persisting, pushing the heat index over 105F over all but \nthe far N, and perhaps over 110F over our far SE. If confidence in \nthis continues to grow, additional Heat Advisories will be needed, \nand an Extreme Heat Watch or Warning may be considered in some \nareas for Mon. \n\nTemps will remain above normal with high humidity through mid week, \nbut with slightly lower intensity. The mid level ridge to our S will \ngradually retrograde in response to a southward progression of the \nflat NW flow from the Midwest through VA/NC, nudging thicknesses \ndown a bit, although our SW areas may stay atypically hot into late \nweek.\n\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... A Marginal (level 1 of 5) risk of severe \nthunderstorms continues through this evening over all of central NC, \nand over the north Sunday. Damaging winds are the primary threat. \n\nFor another day, we're in a regime featuring high PW aoa 2\" and mdt-\nhigh SBCAPE of 1500-3000 J/kg, highest in the S and far W, but with \nmodest mid level lapse rates and poor deep layer bulk shear, \nparticularly in the area of highest CAPE, resulting in low overlap \nof the most favorable CAPE/shear. Predictability of precise location \nand timing of convection remains somewhat low beyond the first 24 \nhours, as the primary features that are focusing convection remain \nmultiple weak outflows from upstream convective systems, along with \nthe sea breeze and terrain-favored storms rolling into the W \nPiedmont. The sub-20-kt deep layer shear will continue to limit \nstorm organization and longevity, but a few strong storms producing \nwater-loaded downdrafts remain possible through mid evening. This \noverall regime will hold for Sun as well, primarily (but not solely) \nacross the north as outflows drift in from the NW and N, and where \nPWs are expected to be a bit higher as compared to our SW sections.",
